\image(figures/flash_tail.svg)
// result_end
\caption(After classification the first four positions form a correct class range but contain three strict inversions. The measured insertion shifts equal that inversion count. The vertical line marks the class boundary.)
\alttext(A five-position plot compares original values, actual post-carry values and final ascending values. A boundary before the last slot separates the four-value class from the maximum.)

> What the work bound does, and does not, say

Counting and cumulative ends cost O(n+m). Carry destinations are consumed once and the
leader index only increases, so the carry phase has linear work. The last phase is a
single whole-array insertion loop. Because ordered classes do not interleave in value,
strict inversions occur only within a class. Hence

$$
\text{insertion shifts}=\operatorname{inv}(\text{post-carry array})
\le\sum_k\frac{c_k(c_k-1)}2,
\qquad T=O\left(n+m+\sum_k c_k^2\right).
$$
\alttext(Insertion shifts equal the strict inversions after the carry phase and are bounded by the sum of within-class pairs. Total work is bounded by input length, class count and squared class occupancies.)

The comparison-only lower bound does not forbid value arithmetic and indexing.
Nevertheless, Flashsort's finishing comparisons can be quadratic when distinct values
concentrate in one class. Occupancy gives an upper bound; it does not specify their
actual order or count only distinct keys. All-equal inputs instead take an O(n) shortcut
without a class vector or insertion tail.

For an ideal model with independent assignments to fixed class probabilities p_k,
E(sum c_k²)=n+n(n−1)sum p_k². Balanced probabilities over Θ(n) classes give a linear
expectation. This model explains the mechanism; it does not prove a blanket claim for
arbitrary clustered inputs, rounded arithmetic, or every distribution normalised by
sample-dependent extrema. The experiments below measure actual work instead of using
flat bars as a runtime certificate.

The ordinary implementation stores m integer class boundaries. With m≈0.43n this is
Θ(n) auxiliary metadata, even though the carry permutation mutates the original array.
Owned trace snapshots add further O(n+m) storage. Neither is constant-space sorting.

> Preserve both original examples and aggregate the classes actually used

Keep the original scrambled fifteen values. The second example retains the local
mt19937_64 seed 11, inclusive integer distribution 0 through 9999, and sample size 2000.
The native sample helper reproduces the original program on the same standard library;
no promise is made about distribution mappings across different toolchains.

The old plot computed floor(19(v−min)/(max−min)) directly from values. Those are a
separate set of twenty bins, not an aggregation of the sorter's 860 classes. In particular,
its bin 19 contains only maximum values for this integer sample. The new plot sums
consecutive ranges of 43 actual class counts. All 2,000 observations are retained.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/flash_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <climits>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
const auto tiny=dm::algorithms::flashsort_trace(std::vector<int>{29,4,17,88,41,6,73,50,12,95,33,61,8,24,77});
  std::print("Original 15 input: {}.\nOriginal 15 sorted: {}.\nOriginal 15 actual counts {}; carry writes {}; insertion shifts {}.\n\n",tiny.input,tiny.output,tiny.class_counts,tiny.work.permutation_writes,tiny.work.insertion_shifts);
  const auto sample=dm::algorithms::flashsort_uniform_sample();const auto t=dm::algorithms::flashsort_trace(sample);const auto groups=dm::algorithms::flashsort_class_groups(t.class_counts,20);
  std::print("| display group | actual class interval | total observations |\n| ---: | --- | ---: |\n");std::vector<double>gx,gy;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<groups.size();++i){const auto&g=groups[i];std::print("| {} | [{},{}) | {} |\n",i,g.begin,g.end,g.count);gx.push_back(i);gy.push_back(g.count);}
  const auto maximum_count=std::count(sample.begin(),sample.end(),t.maximum);
  std::print("\nOriginal sample: n {}; minimum {}; maximum {}; classes {}; nonempty {}; largest class {}; occupancy-square sum {}.\n",sample.size(),t.minimum,t.maximum,t.work.class_count,t.work.nonempty_classes,t.work.max_occupancy,t.work.occupancy_square_sum);
  std::print("Actual carries {}; insertion comparisons {}; shifts {}; pair upper bound {}; sorted {}.\n",t.work.permutation_writes,t.work.insertion_comparisons,t.work.insertion_shifts,(t.work.occupancy_square_sum-t.work.n)/2,std::is_sorted(t.output.begin(),t.output.end()));
  std::print("Old value-bin 19 contains only {} maximum observation(s); the last actual class group contains {} observations.\n",maximum_count,groups.back().count);
  std::print("Ordinary class-vector payload: {} int entries / {} bytes; input payload {} bytes, excluding allocator overhead.\n",t.work.class_count,t.work.class_count*sizeof(int),sample.size()*sizeof(int));
  std::vector<double>x,original,sorted;for(std::size_t i=0;i<tiny.input.size();++i){x.push_back(i);original.push_back(tiny.input[i]);sorted.push_back(tiny.output[i]);}
  auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();a.points(x,original,"Original",{234,88,12},5).line(x,sorted,"Sorted",{37,99,235},2).title("Original fifteen-value example").x_label("Position").y_label("Value").y_limits(0,125).legend({"Original","Sorted"},{{234,88,12},{37,99,235}});
  auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();b.bars(gx,gy,"Actual observations",{234,88,12}).title("860 classes, grouped 43 at a time").x_label("Consecutive class group").y_label("Total observations").x_limits(-.6,19.6).y_limits(0,150);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,540).title("Original data with the sorter's actual class aggregation").fit_panels().save_svg("build/flash_original.svg");
  if(t.work.class_count!=860||t.work.insertion_shifts!=1058||t.work.occupancy_square_sum!=6742)throw std::runtime_error("original sample work");
}
