// result_end
\caption(The full int range and two formerly unsafe finite double ranges now sort with valid class indices. Nonfinite input and impossible signed lengths are rejected, and whole capture limits preserve owned evidence.)
\alttext(A native flow diagram shows input validation and a single extrema scan, a safe integer or scaled-floating classifier, and the shared carry and insertion kernel with optional owned records.)

> What this establishes

The original sorted outputs and sample are preserved, while the plot now represents actual
sorter classes. Tiny traces show the carried values and the difference between class order
and full numeric order. Measured shifts support the inversion identity and expose a
quadratic family with the same occupancies as an easier family. Finite-range validation
removes observed undefined behavior without changing the historical safe path. These
checks establish specific arithmetic and algorithmic properties; they do not establish
that this sort is fastest on any processor or that arbitrary skewed data has linear work.
No external dataset is needed for these controlled sorting questions.

> References

- K.-D. Neubert, [The FlashSort Algorithm](https://www.neubert.net/FSOIntro.html), author introduction and publication links: euroFORTH'97 and Flashsort1 Algorithm, Dr. Dobb's Journal, February 1998,p123. Its class-vector/cycle mechanism is the starting point; the native variant here uses m≈0.43n and a whole-array insertion tail.
- K.-D. Neubert, [Flash-Sort: Sorting by in situ Permutation](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~deppeler/cs400/readings/Sorting/flashpap.pdf), especially the class-pointer and cycle-leader construction. The paper's byte-key example is not a universal complexity guarantee for this finite arithmetic value classifier.
- K.-D. Neubert, [Collection of FlashSort Implementations](https://www.neubert.net/Flacodes/FLACodes.html), author-hosted implementations and variants. Maximum relocation and recursive variants differ from the retained native cycle order.

